\details{
\subsection{Coordinate validation}{

Coordinate validation is performed with CoordinateCleaner. Available tests
are:
\itemize{
\item \code{capitals}: records at country capital coordinates
\item \code{centroids}: records at country or province centroids
\item \code{equal}: records with identical latitude and longitude values
\item \code{gbif}: records matching known GBIF geospatial issues
\item \code{institutions}: records at known herbarium or museum coordinates
\item \code{outliers}: geographic outliers within a species range
\item \code{seas}: records located in marine areas for terrestrial species
\item \code{zeros}: records at coordinates \verb{(0, 0)}
}

The \code{seas} test is evaluated against the bundled \code{WorldLandMap} land
polygons buffered by 5 km. The buffer absorbs records that a coarse
coastline places just offshore, which would otherwise be reported as sea
records; it is applied because the map is a low-resolution outline, not
because those records are expected to be at sea.
}

\subsection{Parallel processing}{

Records with complete coordinates are chunked across the requested number of
workers and validated with \code{foreach} and \code{doParallel}. The worker count is
capped to the number of species and of records, so no worker is left idle.

Chunks are built from species, not from consecutive rows: every record of a
species is kept in one chunk, and whole species are assigned to the chunk
that currently holds the fewest records, which keeps chunk sizes close to
equal. Records of a species must not be split across workers because the
\code{outliers} test judges each record against the distribution of its own
species, so splitting them would change which records are flagged.
}

\subsection{Verdict columns}{

\code{CoordinateProblematic} gains one logical column per test listed in \code{tests},
named after the corresponding CoordinateCleaner flag (\code{.cap} for \code{capitals},
\code{.cen} for \code{centroids}, \code{.equ} for \code{equal}, \code{.gbf} for \code{gbif}, \code{.inst} for
\code{institutions}, \code{.otl} for \code{outliers}, \code{.sea} for \code{seas}, and \code{.zer} for
\code{zeros}). \code{TRUE} means the record passed that test, \code{FALSE} means it failed
it, and records that never reached validation keep \code{NA} throughout, which is
how coordinate-less records can be told apart from flagged ones. These
columns are appended after the occurrence columns. \code{CoordinateCleaned} holds
records that passed every test and keeps the occurrence columns only.
}

\subsection{Empty input}{

If no records have complete coordinates, validation is skipped. An empty
\code{CoordinateCleaned} table is returned, while records lacking coordinates are
placed in \code{CoordinateProblematic}, with every verdict column set to \code{NA}.
}
}
